<h1>Redoubt&rsquo;s F-Droid repository</h1>
<p class="lede">Redoubt runs its own F-Droid repository. It serves the same APKs as the GitHub release, signed with the same key, and F-Droid tells you when a new version is out.</p>

<h2 id="add">Add the repository</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Install the F-Droid app from <a href="https://f-droid.org/">f-droid.org</a> if you do not have it.</li>
  <li>On the phone, open this link: <a href="fdroidrepos://@REPO_HOSTPATH@?fingerprint=@FINGERPRINT@">add Redoubt&rsquo;s repository to F-Droid</a>. Or scan the QR code below with F-Droid (<em>Repositories &rarr; +</em>), or type the address and fingerprint there by hand.</li>
  <li>Check that F-Droid shows the fingerprint below, then add the repository and install <strong>Redoubt</strong> from it.</li>
</ol>
<p><img src="fdroid-qr.svg" alt="QR code that adds Redoubt's F-Droid repository with its fingerprint" width="260" height="260"></p>

<h3 id="address">Repository address</h3>
<pre><code>@REPO_URL@</code></pre>
<h3 id="fingerprint">Repository fingerprint</h3>
<pre class="fingerprint"><code>@FINGERPRINT_SPACED@</code></pre>
<p>This is the fingerprint of the key that signs the repository&rsquo;s index, not of the app. F-Droid refuses an index signed by any other key once you have added the repository with it.</p>

<h2 id="app-key">The app inside is the same APK</h2>
<p>The repository does not rebuild or re-sign Redoubt. Each APK in it is byte for byte the file on the <a href="https://github.com/CPlusPlus17/Redoubt/releases/latest">GitHub release</a>, and its signing certificate has the same SHA-256 as every other Redoubt APK; see <a href="install.html#verify">Verify the download</a>. An APK installed from GitHub and one installed from this repository update each other in place.</p>
<p>F-Droid offers the APK for your phone&rsquo;s processor (64-bit ARM, 32-bit ARM or x86_64). The universal APK is only on the GitHub release.</p>

<h2 id="archive">Older releases</h2>
<p>The repository shows the newest @KEEP_VERSIONS@ releases. Every older release stays available in its archive section, <code>@ARCHIVE_URL@</code>, signed with the same key. To see it in F-Droid, open <em>Repositories</em>, select Redoubt&rsquo;s repository, open <em>Settings</em> and turn on <em>Show archived apps and outdated versions of apps</em>; on Redoubt&rsquo;s app page, pick <em>Redoubt (archive)</em> in the repository list to see the older versions. Installing an older version over a newer one is a downgrade, which Android allows only after uninstalling the app, and that removes its data.</p>

<h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
<p>F-Droid notifies you of new versions. Redoubt&rsquo;s own opt-in <em>Check for updates</em> switch is not needed: from the release after 157.0-2, Redoubt hides it when F-Droid installed or last updated the app. On 157.0-2, leave it off.</p>
<h2 id="hosting">Where the repository is hosted</h2>
<p>F-Droid downloads the index and the APKs from <code>@REPO_HOST@</code>, a Hetzner Object Storage bucket in @LOCATION_NAME@ that this project rents from Hetzner Online GmbH. Hetzner sees your IP address and the time of each request. The address is Hetzner&rsquo;s, not <code>redoubtbrowser.org</code>, because Hetzner&rsquo;s object storage cannot serve a bucket under its own domain; the fingerprint above, not the address, is what proves the repository is Redoubt&rsquo;s. This page itself is hosted on GitHub Pages.</p>
<p>This repository is not f-droid.org&rsquo;s main repository. Redoubt is not there: f-droid.org would build and sign the app with its own key, which would give it a different fingerprint from every other Redoubt APK.</p>
